! Look-ahead / instruction-unit overlap benchmark (docs/
! microarchitecture_plan.md stage 3b, the folklore item "I-box time
! can be covered largely by neighboring floating point operations",
! Perf. Char. p.3-17). Ten iterations of: load SUM, add ONE, store
! SUM, then V+ on X2 (index arithmetic, 8 I-box cycles = 16 clocks)
! and CB on X1 (16 clocks taken). E-box work per iteration: three
! full-word SAU operations at 24 clocks each = 72 clocks, and the
! store's word is the next iteration's load, so that load waits for
! the store. With I-box run-ahead the V+ and CB (32 clocks of I-box
! time) hide behind the E-box: about 10*72 + start-up ~ 800 clocks.
! Serialized (I-box waiting for the levels to drain) they add ~32 per
! iteration: ~1120. Measured 807. The bound of 900 in run_tests.sh
! separates them. SUM is CNOP-aligned so the check reads one word.
START   LCI, $1, 10
        LVI, $2, 0
LOOP    L(BU,64,8), SUM
        +(BU,64,8), ONE
        ST(BU,64,8), SUM
        V+, $2, STEP
        CB, $1, LOOP
DONE    BEW, $
        CNOP
SUM     DD(BU,64,8), 0
ONE     DD(BU,64,8), 1
STEP    VF, 1.0
        END, START
